<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizar ibn al-Mustansir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1045–<a href="/years/1095">1095</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a> ibn al-Mustansir was the eldest son of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustansir. On his father's death in 1094 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> passed him over in favour of his younger brother al-Musta'li. Nizar rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> but was defeated, imprisoned and put to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1095. His supporters, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-i-sabbah">Hasan-i Sabbah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, recognised him and his line as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, founding the Nizari branch of Ismailism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizar-ibn-al-mustansir.jpg" alt="Nizar ibn al-Mustansir" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr Bassam Zahra · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1a_Fatimid_Coin_of_Imam_Nizar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
